    "chunk_size_bytes": 1940,
    "chunk_text": "G7 Secret Club Meets Its Boss\n\nWell.. \u201cI\u2019m the boss\u201d, he joked, but the room understood the message without needing translation. Donald Trump had arrived at the G7 carrying something far more powerful than a prepared diplomatic brief. He carried uncertainty. The summit had already been moved by a day to accommodate his 80th birthday celebrations at the White House, attended by around 4,000 people, including senior administration figures, wealthy donors, technology leaders and active military personnel. While the celebration projected strength and loyalty at home, the world\u2019s most powerful democratic leaders were adjusting their own timetable around the man who had returned to the centre of the alliance he once openly criticized.\n\nThe image was extraordinary. Leaders representing some of the richest economies on earth were waiting. The organization created to coordinate the global economy was adapting itself around one personality. A summit that was supposed to represent collective leadership became a demonstration of hierarchy. Nobody announced that the rules had changed. Nobody needed to. The behaviour of the participants revealed it.\n\nThe G7 has always carried this tension between appearance and reality. It presents itself as a gathering of democratic partners working for global stability, but its origins were far more exclusive. It began not as a universal institution but as a private conversation among powerful states facing economic crisis. In the early 1970s, after the collapse of the Bretton Woods monetary system and the oil shock that shook industrial economies, finance ministers and central bankers from leading economies began informal discussions known as the Library Group. The name itself reflected the quiet nature of the meetings. They were not public assemblies. They were conversations among officials who believed they had the economic weight to solve problems affecting the wider world."
