    "chunk_text": "The dispute over Iran showed the same pattern. Trump promoted the agreement as a breakthrough, while critics argued that the arrangement offered Tehran a significant economic lifeline. The framework reportedly included sanctions relief mechanisms, renewed oil exports, access to frozen funds and discussions around a major reconstruction programme. Supporters argued that restrictions on Iran\u2019s nuclear ambitions represented progress. Opponents argued that the deal rewarded a government that had survived years of international pressure without fundamentally changing its strategic behaviour.\n\nChina\u2019s response to the G7\u2019s critical minerals strategy revealed another major shift. The declaration avoided naming Beijing directly in every sentence, but the message was clear. The G7 believes that dependence on one supplier for essential resources creates a strategic vulnerability.\n\nRare earth elements and permanent magnets are not ordinary commodities. They are embedded in the technologies that define modern power. China\u2019s dominance does not come only from mining. Its strength lies in processing, refining and manufacturing. The West discovered that it had allowed another country to control important parts of the industrial foundation of the future.\n\nBeijing responded by accusing the G7 of creating exclusive economic blocs and violating free trade principles. The accusation has gained attention because both sides now use similar language to defend opposite actions. China describes export controls as protection of national security. Western governments describe diversification and restrictions as protection against economic pressure. Each side sees itself as responding defensively.\n\nThe digital declaration from the summit revealed another battlefield. The G7\u2019s proposals on protecting children online, artificial intelligence safety and algorithm transparency appear focused on social responsibility, but they also represent a struggle over control of the digital environment."
