    "chunk_text": "In simple words without any economic jargon, they were saying that we need a revolution to make the poor less poor .I do not know whether they knew anything about Chairman Mao in China. He too had none of the slowly, slowly, catch the monkey approach. He rushed to destroy the then prevailing economic moulds, that ensured ,that wealth remained with the rich, whilst the poor remained poor. Post revolution led by Mao they created a new economic mould that would ensure the creation of an egalitarian society.\n\nHo Chi Minh then a highly respected Marxist was the only one who adopted a step by step approach to change their then existing mould in Vietnam.\n\nIn most places the head of the business earned about four times the salary of the lowest paid. It was a small sample from the places I was taken to by my guide who was a young Communist, but it gave the flavour of the wage structure.\n\nWe are a country of 1000\u2019s of villages. As we travel a few miles from any major town we begin to see the familiar rural scenario .That\u2019s the heartland of our country. That\u2019s also the heartland of the poor. To create a more prosperous country we have to create prosperous rural communities .Creating a few scattered prosperous urban pockets in rural Sri Lanka will not be the pathway for creating a prosperous country.\n\nHe is the only politician that was emotionally committed to bring some economic prosperity to rural Sri Lanka. His intention was to create employment opportunities in the rural areas by bringing industry to the rural countryside. Sadly he was killed and we did not get a picture of the full scenario he had in mind. His vision (we guess) was perhaps to create prosperous villages, and help the poor to be less poor. It was probably very different from a vision of using the rural poor to provide cheap labour for industry.."
