    "chunk_text": "Group-stage draws are allowed and even common. Knockout football does not give that luxury. If a round-of-32 match is level after 90 minutes, the game goes into extra time, played as two 15-minute halves for a total of 30 additional minutes. If the scoreline is still tied after that, the match is decided by a penalty shootout. This format applies through every knockout round, including the final, so a World Cup winner could theoretically be crowned on penalties at MetLife Stadium come 19 July.\n\nWe refresh it daily through the knockout rounds, filling in confirmed teams, scores and winners as each match finishes. It is updated by hand, not second-by-second live.\n\nThe round of 32 begins on 28 June 2026 and runs through 4 July, with all sixteen matches played across stadiums in the United States, Canada and Mexico.\n\nThirty-two teams qualify: the top two finishers from each of the twelve groups, plus the eight best-ranked third-placed teams from across the tournament.\n\nA team qualifies by finishing first or second in its group, or by finishing third and ranking among the eight best third-placed teams across all twelve groups once every group match has been played.\n\nThe match goes to 30 minutes of extra time, split into two 15-minute halves. If the score is still level after that, a penalty shootout decides which team advances.\n\nThe final is scheduled for Sunday, 19 July 2026 at MetLife Stadium in East Rutherford, New Jersey, and will be the 104th and final match of the tournament.\n\nFIFA World Cup 2026 Group Stage Tables: Live Standings, Rankings and Qualification Guide\n\nFIFA World Cup 2026 Results: Every Match from Group Stage to Final\n\nWhich teams have qualified for the FIFA World Cup 2026 knockouts, round of 32?"
